# XSH pread()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The page is shared with read() (module xsh/read, which also tests
# pread() as XSH.read.015 to .017). This module tests pread() on its own:
# what "equivalent to read() except for the position" means in each case.
# Behaviour that is read()'s alone (terminals, pipes, sockets, signals) is
# tested in xsh/read. Error tests arrange exactly one error condition
# (XSH 2.3).

assertion  XSH.pread.001
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pread(fd, buf, n, off) on a regular file returns the n bytes
           starting at offset off, whatever the descriptor's file offset
           is, and does not change that file offset (set to 2, a pread()
           at 6, then a read() returns the bytes from 2).
test       t/001-offset.c

assertion  XSH.pread.002
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pread.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       A pread() whose range crosses the end of file returns only the
           bytes before it; one starting at the end of file, and one
           starting beyond it, return 0.
test       t/002-eof.c

assertion  XSH.pread.003
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() with nbyte 0 on a valid descriptor of a regular file
           returns 0 and has no other result.
test       t/003-zero.c

assertion  XSH.pread.004
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() of a range of a regular file that was never written but
           lies before the end of file (the file extended by
           ftruncate()) reads as bytes of value zero.
test       t/004-hole.c

assertion  XSH.pread.005
anchor     functions/pread.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() fails with EBADF on a descriptor that is not open, and
           on one open only for writing.
test       t/005-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.pread.006
anchor     functions/pread.html#ERRORS/ESPIPE
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() on the read end of a pipe holding data fails with
           ESPIPE.
note       Whether data is consumed by the failed call is not checked: a
           failed call may have been partly performed (XSH 2.3).
test       t/006-espipe-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.pread.007
anchor     functions/pread.html#ERRORS/ESPIPE
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() on a FIFO holding data, opened for reading, fails with
           ESPIPE.
note       As XSH.pread.006.
test       t/007-espipe-fifo.c

assertion  XSH.pread.008
anchor     functions/pread.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() on a regular file with a negative offset fails with
           EINVAL and the file offset is unchanged.
note       See SI-3 (the "offset unchanged" sentence is checked anyway).
test       t/008-einval.c

assertion  XSH.pread.009
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() is not affected by O_APPEND: on a descriptor that has
           it, pread() still reads from the offset given and leaves the
           file offset alone.
test       t/009-append.c

assertion  XSH.pread.010
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pread() works with an offset that does not fit in 32 bits: data
           written with write() at 2^32 + 5 is read back at that offset.
note       Needs a 64-bit off_t and a file system that holds the file
           (sparse); otherwise the setup is UNRESOLVED or NOTINUSE.
test       t/010-large-offset.c

assertion  XSH.pread.011
anchor     functions/pread.html#ERRORS/EISDIR
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2008
says       XSI: pread() on a directory either reads (if the
           implementation allows it) or fails with EISDIR.
test       t/011-directory.c

assertion  XSH.pread.012
anchor     functions/pread.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pread.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       Successive pread() calls at the same offset return the same
           data, and a pread() sees what write() just put there (through
           another descriptor of the same file).
test       t/012-repeat-and-see.c
